\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\taxon{Definition}

\title{Cubies, sticker states, and fixed local frames}

\p{A fixed-frame description of a three-layer cube needs three pieces of
vocabulary. A \vocabk{cubie}{frap-0003} is one physical unit of the layered
cube, with a home position and the visible stickers incident to that
position. For a three-layer cube these are the eight corners, twelve edges
and six centres described in [how the cube models connect](frap-001A).
Bonzio, Loi and Peruzzi use this cubie decomposition in their general model
\citet{Sections 2--3}{bonzio2018nxnxn}; the fixed-frame, visible-sticker
formulation here is our adaptation to the present cube convention.}

\p{For #{N=3}, let #{\mathcal S} be the 54 labelled visible stickers and let
#{\mathcal P=\{(p,n):p\in\{-1,0,1\}^3,\ \lVert p\rVert_\infty=1,\ n
\text{ is an incident outward normal at }p\}}. Thus #{\lvert\mathcal
P\rvert=54}. A \newvocab{sticker state} is a bijection
#{s:\mathcal S\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal P}: every valid surface slot has
exactly one occupant. The solved assignment #{s_0} is the reference state,
and #{s_0^{-1}\mathbin{\circ}s} records the state as a permutation of the
54 slots; a move composes this permutation with its induced slot
permutation. Marchetti's position-and-label transport gives the structural
pattern \citet{Section 3.1, Definition 7 and equation (5)}{marchetti2025rubik};
the present 54-slot bijection is our adaptation of that pattern to the
three-layer cube. The correspondence between these slots, cubie fibres, and
the coordinates #{(\sigma,x;\tau,z)} is made explicit in
[how the cube models connect](frap-001A).}

\p{A \newvocab{fixed local frame} specializes the
\vocabk{local frame}{frap-000Q}: it is a chosen reference ordering of the
incident sticker directions at each cubie. A move records both the cubie's
destination and how its labels are transported relative to that ordering.
The six centre directions keep the global viewing frame fixed; a whole-cube
rotation would change that frame and is a separate symmetry. These
definitions distinguish a labelled state from a colour observation, which
forgets labels that look alike.}
